From c1ad26f0a2187b493c52be52d22507b8a646270d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 19:30:37 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Prose Portal over a virtio device; the TCP daemon is gone

The portal carried by TCP was the wrong design and it did not work: a
network daemon is a second system with its own failure modes, it gave the
guest a listening port it never needed, and on the installed build every
connection completed its handshake and was then met with silence --
still, after a thread per connection -- for reasons that could not be
seen from outside. The guest should talk to the host application through
a device, as the display and the MIDI port already do.

A custom virtio device, ID 61, modelled on prose_midi down to where the
module lives: under drivers/audio/hmulti, because a virtio child's driver
is searched only in the context of the /dev/<class> scan that first
probes its PCI class, and multimedia is the class whose scan is proven to
happen. /dev/misc is taught to the device manager as /dev/midi was in
0023, so opening the device directly probes the same devices. The jam
target is <driver>prose_portal: the daemon is a target of the same name,
jam targets are global, and without the grist the two rules merged into
one link with both kinds of glue and no main().

The driver publishes /dev/misc/prose/portal/0 as a datagram device: one
read() is one whole request, one write() one whole frame. Buffers the
daemon has not read stay with the driver, so the host can have at most
eight requests outstanding -- that is the flow control, and the only
place anything waits. One opener, so two daemons cannot each see half the
frames.

The daemon answers each request on its own thread, so a long command
delays no ping, and a result longer than a frame goes out as RESULT then
RESULT_MORE frames with the same id, whole frames back to back under a
lock. No socket, no port, nothing to hold open, nothing a caller can jam
by connecting and going quiet. If the device is not there, the owner of
the Mac has not allowed automation, and the daemon exits quietly.

Verified on a fresh boot: the daemon says hello at 1.9 s, before there is
a desktop; a ping sent one second into a six-second command is answered
at once; a 150000-byte result arrives as five frames and reassembles to
exactly 150000 bytes.

